\section{Implementation and Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:impl}

\subsection{Implementation}

All algorithms were implemented from scratch in Python~3.12. Using
library functions like \texttt{sorted()} was deliberately avoided so
that each implementation is transparent and can be independently
verified. The only exception is Tim\-Sort, where we implemented the
core structure (run detection and merge logic) but relied on Python's
built-in sort for the Insertion Sort sub-routine used on short runs,
since reimplementing that part would not add anything meaningful.

The full source code is at:
\begin{center}
\url{https://github.com/maksatkerimov10-sys/MPI-paper}
\end{center}

A short excerpt showing the Quicksort implementation with
median-of-three pivot selection:

\begin{lstlisting}[caption={Quicksort with median-of-three pivot.}]
def _median_of_three(arr, lo, hi):
    mid = (lo + hi) // 2
    if arr[lo] > arr[mid]:
        arr[lo], arr[mid] = arr[mid], arr[lo]
    if arr[lo] > arr[hi]:
        arr[lo], arr[hi] = arr[hi], arr[lo]
    if arr[mid] > arr[hi]:
        arr[mid], arr[hi] = arr[hi], arr[mid]
    # now arr[mid] is the median; place it at hi-1
    arr[mid], arr[hi-1] = arr[hi-1], arr[mid]
    return arr[hi-1]

def _quicksort(arr, lo, hi):
    if hi - lo < 2:
        return
    pivot = _median_of_three(arr, lo, hi)
    i, j = lo, hi - 1
    while True:
        i += 1
        while arr[i] < pivot: i += 1
        j -= 1
        while arr[j] > pivot: j -= 1
        if i >= j: break
        arr[i], arr[j] = arr[j], arr[i]
    arr[i], arr[hi-1] = arr[hi-1], arr[i]
    _quicksort(arr, lo, i - 1)
    _quicksort(arr, i + 1, hi)

def quicksort(arr):
    _quicksort(arr, 0, len(arr) - 1)
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{Benchmark Design}

\paragraph{Input sizes.}
We tested $n \in \{1\,000,\ 5\,000,\ 10\,000,\ 50\,000,\ 500\,000\}$.
The largest size (500\,000) was only feasible for the $O(n \log n)$
algorithms; the $O(n^2)$ algorithms were not run beyond 50\,000 because
they would have taken hours.

\paragraph{Input distributions.}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Random}: uniformly random integers in $[0,\, 10^6]$,
          generated with \texttt{random.randint}.
    \item \textbf{Sorted}: the same values in ascending order.
    \item \textbf{Reverse sorted}: the same values in descending order.
    \item \textbf{Nearly sorted}: a sorted array with 2\% of positions
          randomly swapped.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Measurement.}
Wall-clock time was recorded using \texttt{time.perf\_counter()}. Each
combination of (algorithm, $n$, distribution) was repeated five times
on newly-generated data; we report the median time. The median is
preferred over the mean because occasional OS scheduling interruptions
can inflate individual measurements.

All tests ran on a laptop with an Intel Core i5-1235U processor,
16\,GB RAM, running Ubuntu~24.04. Python's global interpreter lock
means no parallelism was involved.

\paragraph{Correctness check.}
After every sort, we verified that the output equals
\texttt{sorted(input)} to catch any bugs in the implementations.

