Among the $O(n \log n)$ algorithms, Quicksort is fastest, with
Tim Sort a close second. Heap Sort is noticeably slower than Merge
Sort even though both have the same asymptotic complexity. This is
consistent with published results~\cite{sedgewick2011} and is explained
by Heap Sort's poor cache behaviour: accessing a parent or child in a
heap involves an index calculation that jumps around in memory, whereas
Merge Sort's merge step walks through arrays sequentially.

