\section{Conclusions and Future Work}
\label{sec:conclusions}

We compared ten sorting algorithms across four input distributions and
five sizes. The main conclusions are:

\begin{itemize}
    \item For general-purpose sorting of random data, \textbf{Quicksort
          with median-of-three} is the fastest comparison-based
          algorithm in practice, consistent with its status as the
          industry default for many years.
    \item \textbf{Tim Sort} is the best choice when the input may be
          partially sorted. Its $O(n)$ best case is not merely
          theoretical: we observed sub-millisecond sorting of 500\,000
          elements that were already in order.
    \item \textbf{Insertion Sort} remains competitive for small $n$
          (below roughly 10\,000) on nearly sorted data. This is why
          it is used inside Tim Sort and Intro Sort.
    \item \textbf{Counting Sort and Radix Sort} are faster than
          everything else for integer data, but they cannot handle
          general comparison-based inputs.
    \item \textbf{Bubble Sort and Selection Sort} have no advantage
          in any tested scenario. Bubble Sort does have a nice
          best case for already-sorted data, but Insertion Sort matches
          it there while being significantly faster on average.
    \item \textbf{Heap Sort's} poor cache performance makes it
          noticeably slower than Merge Sort and Quicksort in practice,
          even though all three have the same $O(n \log n)$ complexity.
\end{itemize}

There are several things I would have liked to do but could not fit into
the scope of this work:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{C or C++ implementations}: Python's interpreter
          overhead makes the constant factors much larger than in a
          compiled language. A C implementation would let us see whether
          the relative ordering changes when the overhead is removed.
    \item \textbf{Parallel sorting}: Merge Sort parallelises naturally.
          Comparing parallel implementations would be interesting,
          especially with the Hirschberg parallel sort
          algorithm~\cite{hirschberg1978}.
    \item \textbf{Larger ranges of $k$ for non-comparison sorts}: We
          only used integer keys in $[0, 10^6]$. Testing with much larger
          or smaller ranges would give a clearer picture of when Counting
          Sort stops being practical.
    \item \textbf{Strings and floating-point keys}: All our experiments
          used integers. Sorting strings or floats would involve
          different comparison costs and might change the relative
          performance of algorithms significantly.
\end{itemize}

